 "transcript": {
  "answer": "Yes, the data suggest it is getting somewhat harder. The rise is gradual, and Queensland is still doing slightly better than Australia overall.\n\n**Unemployment rate (seasonally adjusted)**\n- Queensland was 4.53% in September 2026. It was 4.17% in July 2025 and 4.24% in July 2026.\n- Australia was 4.72% in September 2026, up from 4.26% in July 2025.\n- The monthly Queensland figures are noisy. They swung between 3.73% (March and May 2026) and 4.44% (February 2026), so I wouldn't read much into any single month.\n\n**Trend series (smooths out the noise)**\n- Queensland's trend unemployment rate has risen every month since March 2026. It went from 4.12% in March to 4.47% in September 2026.\n- That is about 0.35 percentage points in six months. Before March it was flat at roughly 4.1%.\n\n**Number of unemployed people**\n- Queensland had about 144,400 unemployed people in September 2026. That is up from about 130,000 in July 2025, an increase of roughly 14,300.\n- Nationally, the count rose from about 650,600 to 732,000.\n\n**Employment and participation**\n- Queensland's employment-to-population ratio fell from 64.24% in July 2025 to 63.89% in September 2026. That means a smaller share of the population is in work.\n- Queensland's participation rate was little changed, at 66.92% in September 2026 against 67.03% in July 2025. The rise in unemployment therefore isn't because more people are entering the job market. Employment is simply not keeping pace.\n\nQueensland's unemployment rate is still about 0.2 percentage points below the national rate, but both are moving up.",
